      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To evaluate tolerance to isosorbide dinitrate (ID) in patients with angina pectoris functional class II-III and effect of plasmapheresis in this tolerance management."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "85 patients with angina pectoris functional class II-III taking long-acting ID."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "As shown by repeat transesophageal left atrial pacing, 24.7% patients have developed tolerance to nitrate while taking prolonged ID. Two methods of this tolerance elimination were compared: plasmapheresis + withdrawal of long-acting nitrate and withdrawal of the nitrates only. The former has shown some advantages: use of plasmapheresis reestablished ID efficiency in 4 days (two sessions) in 81.8% and in 7 days (3 sessions) in 100% patients without withdrawal syndrome. The latter method reestablished a marked antiischemic effect of ID in 7 days and 20% patients exhibited a variant of withdrawal syndrome--early negative aftereffect."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "When withdrawal of long-acting nitrates is combined with plasmapheresis in anginal patients to eliminate tolerance to nitrates, the effect is better."
        }
      ]},
